{"parseError":{},"jsonString":"{\n  \"title\": \"The Indian Freedom Struggle: A Century-Long Evolution\",\n  \"intro\": \"Imagine you're part of a massive movement that changes the course of history. The Indian Freedom Struggle was not just a single event, but a century-long journey that transformed a diverse subcontinent into a unified modern nation. Let's explore this incredible story together.\",\n  \"pillars\": [\n    {\n      \"title\": \"The Birth of Resistance (1857-1905)\",\n      \"content\": \"The Indian Freedom Struggle began with the **Revolt of 1857**, often called the 'First War of Independence.' Although it failed, it shattered the myth of British invincibility and led to the transition from East India Company rule to the direct administration of the British Crown. [Action: Draw a timeline on the board to mark this event]. By the late 19th century, the resistance shifted from the battlefield to the political arena with the formation of the **Indian National Congress in 1885**. Initially led by 'Moderates' who sought reform through constitutional dialogue, the movement was radicalized by 'Extremists' like Bal Gangadhar Tilak, who famously declared, 'Swaraj (Self-rule) is my birthright.'\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"The Rise of Non-Violent Resistance (1915-1942)\",\n      \"content\": \"The struggle entered its most transformative phase with the arrival of **Mahatma Gandhi in 1915**, who introduced the revolutionary philosophies of *Satyagraha* (truth-force) and *Ahimsa* (non-violence). Gandhi successfully bridged the gap between the urban elite and the rural masses, turning the independence movement into a true **Jan Andolan** (People's Movement). This era saw three massive waves of resistance: the **Non-Cooperation Movement (1920-22)**, which paralyzed the British administration; the **Civil Disobedience Movement (1930)**, immortalized by the Dandi Salt March; and finally, the **Quit India Movement of 1942**, which issued the ultimate ultimatum of 'Do or Die' to the British Raj. [Action: Draw a diagram to illustrate the three waves of resistance].\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"The Final Push for Independence (1942-1947)\",\n      \"content\": \"Parallel to these non-violent efforts were the militant contributions of revolutionaries like **Bhagat Singh** and the military challenge posed by **Subhas Chandra Bose’s Indian National Army (INA)**. Combined with the economic exhaustion of Britain after World War II and the Royal Indian Navy Mutiny, these internal and external pressures finally forced the British to withdraw, leading to the 'Tryst with Destiny' on **August 15, 1947**, though the triumph was permanently shadowed by the tragic partition of the country. [Action: Show a map of India to highlight the partition].\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"cheatSheet\": \"**Analogies:**\n\n* The Indian Freedom Struggle was like a marathon, where different runners (leaders and movements) took turns to push the movement forward.\n* Gandhi's non-violent resistance was like a drop of water that eventually created a ripple effect, inspiring millions to join the movement.\n\n**Frequently Asked Questions:**\n\n* What was the significance of the Revolt of 1857?\n* How did the Indian National Congress contribute to the freedom struggle?\n* What was the impact of the Quit India Movement of 1942?\",\n  \"discussion\": \"**Student Discussion Prompts:**\n\n* What do you think was the most significant factor that contributed to India's independence?\n* How did the different leaders and movements work together to achieve a common goal?\n* What can we learn from the Indian Freedom Struggle that can be applied to our own lives and struggles?\"\n}"}

